nts":2,"created_at":"2026-08-03T17:04:22Z","updated_at":"2026-10-20T21:22:59Z","closed_at":"2026-10-20T21:22:59Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\nGodot 4.7.stable\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.7.stable - Windows 10 (build 19045) - Multi-window, 3 monitors - OpenGL 3 (Compatibility) - NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2060 (NVIDIA; 32.0.15.9595) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-10700F CPU @ 2.90GHz (16 threads) - 15.77 GiB memory - WASAPI (48000 Hz, Stereo/mono)\n\n### Issue description\n\nWhen using Compatibility renderer with HDR 2D enabled, 3D meshes rendered with additive blending will appear brighter than they should in the editor (this is especially noticeable when the object is black, as in this case it should be invisible). Meshes seem to render correctly at runtime.\n\n**Editor (HDR 2D enabled)**\n<img width=\"1006\" height=\"566\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f27bb99a-a11e-4b69-98a1-138de6b6a9b1\" />\n\n**Editor (HDR 2D disabled)**\n<img width=\"1006\" height=\"566\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/fd43789e-e6e7-495c-b149-33a103ca5b9d\" />\n\n**Runtime**\n<img width=\"1006\" height=\"566\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2d9dc118-ea09-430c-8902-b6f0ce595e5c\" />\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nCreate a project using Compatibility renderer, add a MeshInstance3D and configure it with additive blending, then enable HDR 2D in the project settings.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[compatibility-additive-blend-mrp.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/30670556/compatibility-additive-blend-mrp.zip)","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://av